[agent] Done. The leaky feature is **f6**.
[agent] **How I got there:** The direct HDFS write path (feature-group ingestion on port 8020) was blocked by the local sandbox's network rules — only the HTTPS REST API worked, routed through the environment's localhost proxy (the default `NO_PROXY=10.0.0.0/8` had to be overridden per command since the Hopsworks host is a 10.x address). So I ran the analysis entirely server-side: uploaded `training_data.csv` to `/Resources/leakage` on HopsFS, deployed a Hopsworks Python job (`leakage_analysis`) that computed each feature's association with the label, and read its results back through the platform.
[agent] **Evidence:** f6 alone achieves a perfect single-feature ROC AUC of 1.000, with a 0.993 Pearson correlation to the label and class means 17.4 pooled standard deviations apart — textbook post-outcome leakage. The other five features are ordinary weak predictors (AUC 0.532–0.595, correlations 0.061–0.160).
[agent] **Deliverables:** `submission/answers.json` written locally (with `feature` and `evidence` keys) and published on the platform at `/Resources/submission/answers.json`; full per-feature stats are at `/Resources/leakage/leakage_analysis.json`.
